\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View:Euro Could Weaken Further As Yen Strengthens \b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 23:00\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)- How low can it go? \par \par \uc2  Euro bulls are hoping that question was answered when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged to all-time lows against the dollar, pound, and yen over the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are not so sure the euro has bottomed out. \par \par \uc2  Brief bouts of buying interest helped the euro pick up more than two cents, one pence, and one-and-a-half yen last week, but it's had a hard time holding onto its gains. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect a clearer indication of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-term intentions in the upcoming week with the release of key U.S., U.K. and Japanese data and a meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scheduled. \par \par \uc2  U.S. economic data releases include February retail sales at the start of the week, and both the consumer and producer price indices at the end of the week. The U.K. is releasing inflation measures, retail sales, and employment and wage data. \par \par \uc2  Japan kicks off the week Monday with the release of fourth-quarter gross domestic product figures. The yen's movements Monday will likely set the tone for the week - particularly against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the euro was trading at $0.9642, up from $0.9615 at the start of the New York session and down slightly from $0.9666 late Thursday in New York. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying Y102.29, after dropping about a yen from its intraday high. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was virtually flat at Y106.22, from Y106.20 at the start of the New York session, but down from Y106.59 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  With market players virtually ignoring the mostly positive data coming out of the euro-zone, and hints at near term euro-zone interest rate hikes barely registering a blip in intraday trading, euro watchers have been taking their cue from outside the region. \par \par \uc2  Lately, it's seemed the euro's movements have been determined not by any fundamental data or comments out of the euro zone, but by renewed interest among investors in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's major counterparts. \par \par \uc2  The yen, in particular, has attracted both dollar and euro sellers. Though the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice midweek, buying an estimated $1 to $3 billion to weaken the yen, the action did little to dampen enthusiasm for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Even an expected fourth-quarter GDP contraction has not deterred investors from pumping capital into the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and asset markets. In fact, many analysts say the Japanese yen can only benefit from Monday's release of the 1999 GDP figures. The current consensus is for a 1%-1.2% drop. A smaller number, while still negative, would likely give the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost. \par \par \uc2  In addition, more euro-yen sales are expected in the upcoming week as corporate repatriation continues ahead of the March 31 fiscal year-end. \par \par \uc2  The yen is also being given a boost by an archaic rule requiring Japanese life insurers to report losses in overseas investments if the yen has appreciated 15% or more in the same time period. \par \par \uc2  To avoid having to report those losses, analysts say the life insurers have been hedging or liquidating their positions. The effect is the same in each case: selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen. The euro is likely to continue bearing the brunt of those sales. \par \par \uc2  Analysts are predicting the euro may fall to 100 yen by midweek, while the dollar could slip below 105 yen - at least a yen below levels that prompted the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have not ruled out another move by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in upcoming sessions. \par \par \uc2  "You have to be very careful about the BOJ because they're not looking to see the yen get too strong," said Brian Arabia, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ABN-Amro Bank in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  That might provide some much-needed relief for the euro. In addition, there are increasing expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise rates at its meeting next week, though the anticipated 25 basis-point rate hike will likely be offset by a similar U.S. rate hike at the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  While ECB officials have repeatedly insisted on the euro's "potential to appreciate" - it has yet to be realized. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Credibility is still an issue for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and for the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Bob Lynch, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for Paribas in\par New York. "Even as the data has quite clearly improved, the euro has not\par benefited."\par   -By Jennifer Barrett, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4425\par    email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010805dw3c001oh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
