\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr suffers self-doubt as growth gap narrows. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 07:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 10 (Reuters) - Both the yen and euro gained altitude on Friday as fresh evidence of economic momentum showed Japan and Europe closing the growth gap with the United States. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were particularly intrigued by the dollar's failure to make capital out of a blistering performance from Wall Street on Thursday. The Nasdaq leapt ahead yet the dollar was left in the dust, breaking the recent strong correlation between the two. \par \par \uc2  "Normally a three percent jump in the Nasdaq would have the dollar charging ahead, but instead it's given ground to the euro and that's weird," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The euro was holding at $0.9665 having firmed to $0.9662 in New York on Thursday from a $0.9520 low earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  It was also standing steady at 102.76 yen thanks in part to the promise of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  aid after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted it from lifetime lows around 101.95 this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 106.35 yen in the face of selling by U.S. and Japanese banks, from 106.60 late in New York, so testing levels where the BOJ first stepped into the market on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe there's just a lag in the dollar/stock relationship, or it could be that all this bullish talk we're hearing about Japan and Europe is making investors think again," said the trader. \par \par \uc2  A run of strong data in Japan certainly seemed to have galvanised the authorities, with Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa uncharacteristically unequivocal. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, the economy is clearly starting to get out of recession, and we shouldn't worry," he said. \par \par \uc2  "For someone as circumspect as him, those are clearly fighting words," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  And Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya joined the chorus, declaring that Japan's economy was definitely heading for recovery. \par \par \uc2  Even the ever-cautious BOJ noted an improvement in capital spending, corporate sentiment and profits. The economic data added to the spring-like atmosphere, as trade figures showed imports rising almost 12 percent year-on-year in January, while exports still managed a small gain even in the face of a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts did still expect fourth quarter GDP data due next Monday to show a decline of around 1.0 percent, so technically at least tipping Japan into recession. \par \par \uc2  But they argued that the surprising strength in many leading indicators recently, most notably in capital spending, had made the rather dated GDP data vitually redundant. \par \par \uc2  The buzz out of Europe was also of brightening prospects. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a bullish report on the European economy, predicting GDP growth of above 3.0 percent this year and next. \par \par \uc2  And the data seemed to back that up, with fourth quarter GDP in the eurozone coming in at an annual 3.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  The ECB took a hawkish line on inflation, warning that the risks to price stability were on the upside. That was enough to convince some analysts that the ECB would hike interest rates at its meeting next week, instead of waiting until the end of the month as most had previously expected. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said euro sentiment was also aided by news Deutsche Telekom had withdrawn a bid for two U.S. telecoms companies. There had been reports Deutsche would bid over $100 billion for the two, implying a considerable \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  flow from the euro to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A flood of foreign direct investment out of Europe - some 147 billion euros - was one reason the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fared so badly in 1999. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0601 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   09 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9664   0.9662   +0.02    -4.03   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      106.35   106.60   -0.23    +4.05   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     102.77   103.06   -0.28    -0.32   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6615   1.6628   -0.08    +4.48   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5830   1.5812   +0.11    -2.14   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4557   1.4565   -0.05    +0.71   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6152   0.6155   -0.05    -6.26   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.6067   1.6072   -0.03    +0.19   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6103   0.6107   -0.07    -2.18   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3a04zb4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
