\par\par \b \uc2  Yen bullish but rise curbed by fears of BOJ move. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 03:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 10 (Reuters) - The yen retained a bullish tone by midday in Tokyo on Friday in the wake of recent surprisingly strong economic data and bullish remarks by government officials. \par \par \uc2  But dealers were wary of chasing the yen higher on concerns of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) stepped into the market to buy dollars and euros for yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was firmly supported near 106.20/30 yen on bids from U.S. and Asian operators. That was also the level where the BOJ was seen to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Offers from Japanese exporters were sitting near 107.00 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The euro remained pressured against the yen on selling interest from some Japanese investors before book closings for the end of Japan's financial year on March 31. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 106.57/62 yen compared with 106.60 yen in late U.S. trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 103.09/24 yen versus 103.06 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Trade was slow, with activity largely limited to customer flows before key October-December gross domestic product (GDP) data on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The greenback briefly fell to an intraday low of 106.40 yen as a Japanese non-life insurer was rumoured to have sold Aussie dollars for yen at around 65.50 yen, but dealers said the effect was short-lived. \par \par \uc2  Brightening signs over Japan's fragile economic recovery were putting upward pressure on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese exporters are putting in offers again after recent data and comments," because they are expecting the yen's declines to be limited, a trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, the only thing hindering more yen-buying is the BOJ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by Japanese authorities on Wednesday was in response to rapid fluctuations in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said he had instructed Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ merely acts as the government's agent in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked about October-December GDP to be released on Monday, Miyazawa said: "If it is not good, the January-March GDP will be good. Overall, the economy is clearly starting to get out of recession, and we shouldn't worry." \par \par \uc2  Separately, Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said on Friday that Japan's economy was definitely heading for a recovery, pointing to stronger-than-expected capital expenditure data, improving corporate profits and brisk industrial output. \par \par \uc2  Sakaiya said he still expected October-December GDP to have contracted because of sluggish personal spending. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers brushed off the significance of the data. \par \par \uc2  "The GDP is old data. The key will be the BOJ's 'tankan' survey (in April), especially as we are seeing clear signs of a recovery in capital spending," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "A yen-buying bias will likely remain until then," he added. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ repeated in its monthly report released on Friday that Japan's economy has recently started to improve but that there is still no clear sign of a self-sustaining recovery in private-sector demand. \par \par \uc2  It also said private consumption, which accounts for roughly 60 percent of GDP, remains weak. \par \par \uc2  It did, however, give a slightly brighter view on corporate spending and prices. \par \par \uc2  Japanese investors sold a net 955.2 billion yen worth of foreign bonds in January, MOF data showed on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's current account surplus fell 22.8 percent in January from a year earlier to 610.9 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen was buoyed on Thursday after a MOF survey showed firms cut spending on plant and equipment by 0.7 percent in the October-December quarter from the same period a year earlier, after a much steeper 9.6 percent drop in July-September. \par \par \uc2  That raised speculation that the contraction in October-December gross domestic product would be smaller than has been expected. \par \par \uc2  Bullish sentiment towards the yen was further fuelled after January core machinery orders showed 0.8 percent growth after a 16.1 percent gain in the previous month. January orders were sharply higher from market consensus of 0.7 percent decline. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dipped to a low of 106.17 in New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro was firmer compared than its late New York rate of $0.9662. The euro rose to around $0.9700 overnight on a hawkish monthly report by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  before paring some gains. \par \par \uc2  But the euro remained vulnerable as it failed to break decisively above $0.9700. Large-lot offers were sitting near $0.9700. Some stop-loss buy orders were sighted near $0.9710, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Unless the euro can top parity with the dollar, bearish sentiment for the euro will continue," another city bank dealer said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0245 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   09 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9673   0.9662   +0.11    -3.94   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      106.50   106.60   -0.09    +4.20   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     102.77   103.06   -0.28    -0.32   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6605   1.6628   -0.14    +4.42   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5821   1.5812   +0.06    -2.19   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4550   1.4565   -0.10    +0.66   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6128   0.6155   -0.44    -6.63   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.6069   1.6072   -0.02    +0.21   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6110   0.6107   +0.05    -2.07   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 0245 GMT.  All data taken from Reuters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   09 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9673   0.9662   +0.11    -3.94   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      106.50   106.60   -0.09    +4.20   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     102.77   103.06   -0.28    -0.32   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6605   1.6628   -0.14    +4.42   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5821   1.5812   +0.06    -2.19   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4550   1.4565   -0.10    +0.66   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6128   0.6155   -0.44    -6.63   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.6069   1.6072   -0.02    +0.21   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6110   0.6107   +0.05    -2.07   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par