\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on back foot against yen, euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 10 (Reuters) - The dollar was on its back foot on Friday, outshone by the yen and the euro in the wake of upbeat economic assessments by Japanese and European monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  While wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the dollar's losses against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the greenback also managed to stay a touch above Thursday's one-week lows against the euro, traders were inclined to generally square up long dollar positions ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "Generalised dollar weakness has been as much from profit taking and flows as anything else," said Michael Metcalfe, treasury analyst at NatWest Global Financial Markets in London. "And we have had supportive economic data and supportive comments as well from Japanese and European officials to explain yen and euro strength." \par \par \uc2  In Asian trading, the BOJ said in its monthly report that Japan's economy had recently started to improve. \par \par \uc2  The report came on the heels of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monthly bulletin late on Thursday which forecast continued euro land growth and noted upward pressure on prices had become larger and more protracted. \par \par \uc2  "It has confirmed that it's a matter of when they (the ECB) are going to increase interest rates, rather than if," said Duncan Webster, head of global market research at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 106.23 yen, around levels at which the BOJ was said to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week and barely half a yen above Tuesday's five-week lows. \par \par \uc2  The euro drifted within half a cent below Thursday's $0.9700 one-week peak. \par \par \uc2  WALL STREET PROVIDES LITTLE DOLLAR SUPPORT \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to capitalise from a dramatic rebound in Wall Street stocks late on Thursday, even though the Nasdaq composite ended up three percent and the Dow Jones industrials average closed 1.5 percent higher. \par \par \uc2  "What was typical about it was that the markets are showing an incredible capacity to move sharply in one direction or the other in the final trading hours," Webster at HypoVereinsbank said. "These sort of swings are not having a major effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  any more on a day-to-day basis. \par \par \uc2  Going forward, traders said growing optimism about Japan's economy could mask a likely weak reading in fourth quarter growth data due out next week. \par \par \uc2  Economists were forecasting a contraction in Japan's October-December GDP from the previous quarter, but the BOJ noted in the monthly report an improvement in capital spending, corporate sentiment and profits, while Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa was also uncharacteristically unequivocal. \par \par \uc2  "Overall, the economy is clearly starting to get out of recession, and we shouldn't worry," Miayazawa said. \par \par \uc2  "For someone as circumspect as him, those are clearly fighting words," said a dealer at a European bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3a04tee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
