\par\par \b \uc2  Euro/yen option prices stay lofty despite BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 13:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, March 9 (Reuters) - Lofty euro/yen option prices barely budged on Thursday, as the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week hardly convinced the options market that the euro had seen its bottom against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The euro was trading about 1.5 yen above a 100.90 yen\tab      record low set on Tuesday, but the modest bounce after a 10-percent tailspin in just two weeks has hardly calmed jangled nerves in the options market, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Bank of Japan has come in and put in a little bit of a base underneath (the euro)," said Mark Moor, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  director at Thomson Global Markets. "But I'm not saying it won't be tested again because the market's natural reaction is to stand back a little bit and have another go."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Implied volatility, the market price of options reflecting future volatility in spot rate movement, for one-month at-the-money options \tab     stood at 17.6/17.9 percent, off a quarter point on the day. But that was well above the 14 percent area on February 23, when the spot euro was scaling four-month peaks above 112 yen. Since shorter-dated options are more sensitive to spot market swings, the volatility curve was steeply inverted, with one-year maturity priced at around 16 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Asian trading hours, Japanese Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui said the Bank of Japan had interevened during overseas trading on Wednesday, which took many traders aback as they had believed the BOJ yen-selling had only taken place earlier that day in Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo traders also said the BOJ's yen-selling in Europe was against the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the surprise element kept the market wary of further BOJ action, dealers said the very fact that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action went unnoticed was a potential volatility-boosting factor.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market's perception is that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came in at insignificant levels and was not really aggressive," said Hiroyuki Kobayashi, options trading manager at Sakura Bank in London. "It could be counter-productive." He added that interbank dealers appeared to be generally short in strike prices further down because the spot's recent decline had been so rapid.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That meant those dealers' delta-hedging activity would likely add fuel a downmove in the euro if it resumed its test lower, he added. ((Shin Kishima, London Capital Markets +44-171-542-6721, shinichi.kishima@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3904qwk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
