\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2  as yen hits 5-week high. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 08:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 8 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday for the first time in two months, selling yen for dollars in a bid to protect the country's fragile economy from a resurgent yen after it hit a five-week high against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Government officials confirmed the action, which drove the dollar back up to the previous day's levels, but dealers said Japanese authorities would need to show more resolve if they wanted the dollar and euro to climb even further against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's unexpected foray into the exchange market was expected to help quash attempts by speculative operators to take the yen sharply higher, especially before book-closings as Japan approaches the end of its fiscal year on March 31. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan (BOJ), which acts as the government's agent, was detected buying dollars for yen around the 106.30 to 106.80 yen levels. It was also rumoured buying above 107 yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese vice minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which pushed the dollar up more than one yen to 107.50, and Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said the Finance Ministry had instructed the action. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had hit a five-week low of 105.78 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  "We took appropriate action in the market," Kuroda told reporters. \par \par \uc2  By late Tokyo at 0645 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 107.21/25 yen, against 107.45/48 yen in late Tokyo on Tuesday. The euro was at 102.72/80 yen from around 103.24 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  EURO ADDS PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the BOJ had apparently bought only dollars, not euros, on Wednesday, and Kuroda declined to comment on which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was targeted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro's recent sharp falls against the yen were putting pressure on the dollar against the yen, which otherwise had been supported on contrasting fundamentals between the United States and Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities remain concerned that a stronger yen would hurt exporter earnings and dampen corporate sentiment before domestic demand-led economic activity picks up. \par \par \uc2  Recently, the euro had been pressured against the yen by speculative forces more than actual sales, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Of course there were actual hedge sales and also moves to liquidate the euro-and dollar-denominated assets ahead of the fiscal year," a Japanese commercial bank dealer said. "But except for a life insurer (selling) last week, others were pretty calm." \par \par \uc2  The euro sank to a fresh lifetime low of 100.95 yen in overseas trade on Tuesday, dragging the dollar down to a five-week low of 105.78 yen. \par \par \uc2  "(Japanese) monetary authorities would not want to see the dollar going below 105 yen, and wanted to prevent that before it was too late," said Noriyoshi Tsunoda, manager and chief dealer at Fuji Bank's International Treasury Division. \par \par \uc2  The euro's sharp decline against the yen had raised some market speculation that Japanese authorities would step in to boost the euro against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ did directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defence of the euro on a couple of occasions last year, but the action did not find favour with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was keeping a strictly hands off approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management. \par \par \uc2  Only two days ago, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke flatly stated that short-term measures could not stabilise the euro. And on Tuesday, Miyazawa said the European authorities did not seem too worried by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen on January 4, when the dollar sank to a low of 101.40 yen. ((Tamawa Kadoya, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Wednesday morning, senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said the ministry was monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully and always considering appropriate action to take. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Japanese monetary authorities were seen to be more threatened by the euro's decline against the yen, given the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall of more than 20 percent since its inception last year, and that has been a concern for Japan as it is regarded as a threat to exports and the economy's fledgling recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The real purpose of the monetary authorities was probably to rescue the euro against the yen," Fuji's Tsunoda said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers and analysts have been sceptical about the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro against the yen. They instead expected there would be moves to sustain the dollar against the yen, which would indirectly help lift the embattled European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ did directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defence of the euro on a couple of occasions last year, but the action did not find favour with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was keeping a strictly hands off approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management. \par \par \uc2  Only two days ago Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke flatly stated that short-term measures could not stabilise the euro. And on Tuesday, Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said the European authorities did not seem too worried by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, dealers remained sceptical whether the dollar and the euro could sustain their current levels after the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Ryuichi Takami, manager of Sanwa Bank's Treasury & Trading department, said: "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only helped the dollar to bounce to yesterday's level. I expect the dollar will test its downside again." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 107.45/48 yen in late Tokyo on Tuesday. It now stood at 107.19/29 yen. ((Mariko Hayashibara, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8478 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3804x34\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
