\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  option market wary of yen rise despite BOJ sale. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 14:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  option prices suggested that traders had yet to be convinced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on foreign exchanges on Wednesday, its first in two-months, was enough to curb the yen's gains. \par \par \uc2  This was reflected in the lofty premium which yen call options, the right to buy yen for dollars at a preset price, continued to command over equivalent yen puts, the right to sell yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  The directional preference, known as risk reversals, for one-month options favoured yen calls by 1.25/1.55 percentage points over yen puts, unchanged from Tuesday's European levels. \par \par \uc2  "Risk reversals are fairly well bid for yen calls," said an options trader at a large European bank. \par \par \uc2  "People don't know if the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to stem yen's appreciation and risk reversals are telling us that there is still a risk to the dollar downside." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was said to have sold yen for dollars in Tokyo trade, pushing the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  up by 1.6 percent to around 107.50 yen\tab   from five-week lows around 105.70 hit late on Tuesday.\par The euro also rallied to a high of 103.06 yen\tab      from Tuesday's record lows around 100.90.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda confirmed the BOJ had sold yen but declined to comment about which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it had been against.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts remained sceptical if the euro and and dollar had turned the corner against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was not to turn the tide but rather give yen bulls a note of caution and the market is not leaping on board and naturally chasing dollar/yen, euro/yen higher," said Mark Henry, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at GNI in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People will be prepared to test the BOJ resolve. The risk is now if we do see a break lower again and the Bank of Japan does not come back. People are still wary and front-end volatilities should consolidate."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, the BOJ's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has provided the options market with temporary relief.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Implied volatilities - the market price of options reflecting future spot volatility - pared gains made on Tuesday when the dollar broked below 106.50 and triggered some barrier options, rendering them worthless.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There were large barrier options at 106.50 and there was some concern there. The next one is at 105 strike, but BOJ action suggests that remains a little way off, so people are marking volatilities down," said Henry from GNI.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One-month dollar/yen implied volatility was at 12.3/12.6 percent, down from 12.8/13.1 late in Europe on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Sabrina Ghani, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 8968, sabrina.ghani@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw3804wx8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\pics