\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr, Euro Jump Vs Yen After BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2 909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 06:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar shot up nearly a yen and a half in Tokyo Wednesday after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to head off a sharp appreciation of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro also surged against the yen compared with it levels late in New York late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials confirmed that the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling yen but didn't specify which counter-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was involved. \par \par \uc2  The session began with market sentiment heavily bearish towards the dollar. When traders arrived at their desks, they were greeted with news that U.S. share prices had plunged and the euro had hit a new record low of Y100.95 overnight. Critically, the dollar had also breached key support at Y106.50 in New York. \par \par \uc2  To most market players, those developments spelled doom for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the day ahead. Consequently, interbank players sold the dollar early in the session, pushing it to an intraday low of Y106.02. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  found support around that level, as traders covered short positions amid concern that Japanese financial authorities might take action to head off a further rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Those worries proved to be well-founded. Traders said the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around mid-morning, selling yen and buying dollars first between Y106.30-Y106.40 and then again around Y107.00 and some traders said there were rumors that the BOJ was also buying the euro against the yen. Market participants estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounted to at least $1 billion. Some said it may have been as much as $3 billion. \par \par \uc2  After the BOJ entered the market, the dollar jumped to an intraday high of Y107.50. The euro hit a high of Y103.13, well off its early low of Y101.54. \par \par \uc2  "We took necessary steps in the market today," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  Many traders were confused as to why the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar above Y106.00. On Jan. 4, the last time the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars around Y102 in what appeared to be a bid to keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from crashing below Y100. \par \par \uc2  But most traders believe Wednesday's action was aimed at preventing a slide of the dollar and euro against the yen from sparking a massive wave of stop-loss selling by Japanese institutional investors overly exposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate risk. \par \par \uc2  "The more (the dollar and yen) fall, the more selling it invites. I think the officials wanted to stop that," said Takeo Suzuki, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  While Japanese authorties were successful in putting a floor under the dollar and the euro Wednesday, traders aren't so sure how long the effects will last. \par \par \uc2  "It's not really clear if they changed the market sentiment," Suzuki said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (12:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y107.20, above Y106.09 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y107.85 late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at Y102.73, up from Y101.79 in New York late Tuesday. The euro was at Y103.37 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Market players said that Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't change the underlying forces driving recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  The break of support at Y106.50 overnight has technically cleared the way for another test of the dollar's downside, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, jitters over the future direction of U.S stock markets continue to haunt the dollar. Another sell-off in New York would hurt the dollar against the yen. Tuesday, the Dow Jones Industrial Average sank 374.47 points to close at 9796.03. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, much of yen's strength against the euro stems from continued apathy on the part of European officials toward the euro's slide. \par \par \uc2  "It's not just things that are happening in Japan," Nomura's Suzuki said. \par \par \uc2  As a result, dealers said there is strong demand to sell the dollar on its highs. Initial resistance is pegged at Y107.50. \par \par \uc2  While U.S \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be pressured by offers lined up at the high Y107 level, fear that BOJ will come into the market again is supporting the downside. As a result, the dollar settled down in calm afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  "I think I just want to wait and see if the BOJ continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a Japanese city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  With dollar/yen and euro/yen taking center stage during Wednesday's session, the euro/dollar stuck to narrow ranges, trading between $0.9573-$0.9599. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro was at $0.9583, lower than $0.9595 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $0.9585 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5792, down from $1.5795 in New York late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.5745 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Arran Scott; 813-5255-2929; arran.scott@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates which accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture which is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw38017vx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
