\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Major  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  To Test Ranges This Week \b0\par\par\uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 23:00\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Without a clear indication of directional trends, the market is likely to test recent range boundaries among the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs this week. \par \par \uc2  The euro is expected to move lower, against the yen in particular, but also against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's still a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so the themes of (the past) week should carry into (this) week," said David Gilmore, a partner at Connecticut-based \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  Gilmore agreed with most analysts that the dollar will continue to strengthen against the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the U.K. pound and Swiss franc as well as the euro - but weaken against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Though Japan will release its fourth quarter gross domestic product figures at the start of the week, analysts say the market has already accounted for the projected 0.6%-1.5% contraction and is unlikely to react if the numbers come in as expected. \par \par \uc2  "The market is more likely to respond if we get a smaller than expected decline. There'll be an asymmetrical response,"said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Pittsburgh-based Mellon Financial Corp. \par \par \uc2  The yen should also get a boost from continued repatriation ahead of Japan's March 31 fiscal year-end. \par \par \uc2  After plunging to a new all-time low of $0.9390 last Monday, the euro recovered to trade in the 96 cent to mid-97 cent range by the end of the week. Late Friday, the euro was buying $0.9612, down from $0.9667 Friday morning and from $0.9662 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Though strong chart support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to hold at 96 cents, the euro may bounce lower in the week ahead. \par \par \uc2  "We had a huge move - the market is trying to fish for a new range," said Chandler. \par \par \uc2  The same holds true for the dollar-yen trade. \par \par \uc2  Last Wednesday, the dollar suffered one of its steepest intraday declines yet - dropping more than three yen in about 18 hours. Though it rebounded slightly by week's end, the dramatic drop in dollar/yen has many analysts saying the dollar's 10-yen run may be over. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was trading at Y107.73, up from Y107.65 at the start of the New York session and from Y107.60 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Speculation over rate hikes or \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by European and Japanese central banks set off some of the sporadic movements of the past week. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept open the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to strengthen against the dollar and euro. \par \par \uc2  But European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said late last week that the bank saw no reason to raise rates yet or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He reiterated the widely-held, and oft-cited, belief among ECB officials that the euro had the potential to appreciate over the next several months. \par \par \uc2  The ECB opted last week to leave benchmark rates alone for the time being, though it adopted a tightening bias - an indication of future rate hikes. Many analysts feel rates will be haiked at least 25 basis points by the end of Mrch. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect the Reserve Bank of Australia and the Bank of England's monetary policy committee to leave rates in their respective countries alone when they meet this week. \par \par \uc2  The RBA raised rates by 50 basis points earlier this year, but few believe it will take similar action at Tuesday's meeting. There is talk, however, that the bank will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the Australian dollar falls below 60 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a 17-month low of $0.6045 in the middle of last week after the release of unexpectedly weak retail sales data. It climbed back to the upper 60-cent range Friday after reports that the country's trade deficit had narrowed more than expected in the fourth quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England monetary policy committee members also meet midweek, but earlier comments from MPC officials and softening economic data have diminished expectations of a rate hike. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts say short-sterling futures positions have moved higher\par recently, an indication that investors expect the British \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par weaken further against the dollar.\par    -By Jennifer Barrett; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4425\par    email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010805dw35001ft\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
