\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bests euro as markets cheer U.S. data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 3 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against the euro on Friday as U.S. asset markets applauded a softer-than-expected U.S. employment report that soothed fears the robust economy is growing out of control. \par \par \uc2  The greenback seesawed briefly and headed to fresh session highs against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the government reported U.S. February non-farm payrolls grew by 43,000, following a 384,000-increase in the prior month. \par \par \uc2  The figure fell well below economists' consensus forecasts for a solid 206,000 increase in jobs for February. \par \par \uc2  But the hourly earnings figure - closely watched by the market for signs of wage inflation - met expectations with a 0.3-percent rise. The report showed a slight rise in unemployment to 4.1 percent in February from 4.0 percent in January. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks rallied sharply on the news, carrying the dollar higher against the euro, dealers said. After seesawing briefly as the U.S. session got underway, the dollar headed higher to trade just below 96 cents at 10:20 a.m. (1500 GMT). \par \par \uc2  "Inflation was the big bug-a-boo that was going to throw a wrench into the good times we've had in the U.S., and that was pretty much alleviated with this one number," said Bob Houck, euro trader at Norwest Bank in Minneapolis. \par \par \uc2  "Now, it's just about focusing on the differing growth prospects between the U.S. and European economies, and those definitely favour the U.S. and the greenback," Houck added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the dollar was supported by continued optimism about U.S. economic performance, an outlook strongly in place despite February's soft payrolls number. \par \par \uc2  Despite its gains against the euro, however, the dollar was dragged lower against the yen as dealers selling the euro to buy the yen bid up the price of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 10:20 a.m. (1500 GMT), the dollar was trading near 107.60 yen, slightly below Thursday's 107.75 yen close. The euro was also modestly softer near 103.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the euro had been largely rangebound after mixed results from a battery of euro zone data failed to give much direction and traders remained on hold. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the sentiment for the euro remained bearish and it was unlikely to make much headway in the short-term after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept rates steady on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Only when you have very, very strong comments (from European officials) or perhaps a mild \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of Europe or Asia, then you'll see a short-lived rally for the euro," said John Schein, Fortis vice president. "Like most dealers say: When in doubt, sell." \par \par \uc2  But some dealers added the relatively hawkish remarks by ECB President Wim Duisenberg at a news briefing on Thursday had helped underpin the euro somewhat. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg said on Thursday that the ECB was interested in a strong, stable euro and that a solid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the interest of Europe. \par \par \uc2  Wariness about potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on euro/yen by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the yen's recent gains across the board was keeping the euro propped against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  (( - U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 10:24 A.M. (1524 GMT).  All data \par \par \uc2  taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the \par \par \uc2  daily U.S. close at 4:30 P.M. (2130 GMT). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %   1999\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 02 Mar.\tab \tab \tab Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    0.9625   0.9642   -0.18   -4.42   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     107.68   107.73   -0.05   +5.35   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    103.48   103.75   -0.26   +0.37   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.6710   1.6629   +0.49   +5.08   1.5902\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.5840   1.5759   +0.51   -2.08   1.6176\par Can/dlr\tab \tab     1.4518   1.4557   -0.27   +0.44   1.4455\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab     0.6085   0.6064   +0.35   -7.28   0.6563\par Euro/swiss\tab \tab  1.6072   1.6061   +0.07   +0.22   1.6036\par Euro/stg\tab \tab    0.6071   0.6118   -0.77   -2.69   0.6239\par \par All spots\tab \par Tokyo spots\tab \par Europe spots\tab \par Volatilities\tab \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\tab \par World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Economic Forecasts...     Official rates...\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......     Top events........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Diaries...........   Diaries Index........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Press Digests....    Polls on G7 economies..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Reuters Television.   European markets...... .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw33056if\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pi