\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dlr up vs yen despite Nikkei, euro firmer. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ayumi Moriyama \par \uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 04:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 2 (Reuters) - The dollar gained against the yen in late morning trade on Thursday despite the continued strength of Tokyo share prices, while the euro was off earlier lows after German Finance Minister Hans Eichel showed rare suppport. \par \par \uc2  The dollar recovered after tumbling to a five-week low of 106.55 yen overnight as several players, including Japanese importers and U.S. operators, engaged in a wave of short-covering, but its upside was seen heavy at around 108.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar rose due to demand by Japanese importers ahead of the (0100 GMT) daily fixing, but the main trend has been that of dollar-selling," said one Japanese city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported against both the dollar and yen partly by a euro-friendly comment by Germany's Eichel, which some players took as a sign that chances the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) might raise interest rates at its policy-setting meeting later in the day had risen slightly. \par \par \uc2  "We all believe the markets should pay attention to the fundamentals," the Financial Times quoted Eichel as saying on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "We want a strong euro. Also in terms of its external value." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was quoted at 107.39/42 yen\tab  , up from 107.13 yen in New York late on Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark Nikkei average, which was the main reason to buy yen on Wednesday, rose 0.42 percent by 0225 GMT to 20,165.54 after slipping below the closely watched 20,000 level in early trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro stood at $0.9732/37\tab   and 104.51/60 yen\tab      versus $0.9734 and 104.27 yen in New York late on Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the dollar was vulnerable to selling into rallies, especially by players who were not able to unwind positions overnight when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  skidded against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In addition, looking at the buzzing activity on Tokyo's stock market in the past few days, figures showed that foreign players had once again become buyers in Tokyo shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Nikkei's rise above 20,000 yesterday raised expectations that foreign money would flow into Japanese stocks," said Takahiro Ninomiya, a manager at Toyo Trust & Banking.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Since mid-February, foreign players have sold yen on expectations that Japan's October-December GDP data would show negative growth. What you are seeing now is unwinding of those positions."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Orders for Japanese stocks placed through 13 foreign securities houses before the start of trading on Thursday showed a net buying stance of seven million shares, market sources said. Sell orders were for 35 million shares against buy orders for 42 milion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Ninomiya expected the dollar to trade between 105 and 108 yen until the end of Japan's current fiscal year on March 31.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Regarding the euro, traders said Japanese life insurers continued to re-establish hedges by selling the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Any move below 103.55 yen per euro was likely to lead to a retest of the 102.45 low marked earlier this week, but traders said the market was also wary about the possibility of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  near those levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't think the BOJ would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in dollar/yen at these levels, but it could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the euro breaks below 100 yen, dragging the dollar to around 106.50 yen," another dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Focus was now on the ECB's policy meeting later in the day, where chances of a rate hike seemed to have risen slightly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the majority of dealers believe the ECB will keep interest rates steady, some said tightening could not be ruled out given recent evidence of rising inflation pressures in Europe and Eichel's comments on Thursday. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3-3432-8550 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 0259 GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2130GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   01 Mar.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab     0.9733   0.9734   -0.01    -3.35   1.0070\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab      107.38   107.13   +0.23    +5.06   102.21\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab     104.53   104.27   +0.25    +1.39   103.10\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab    1.6505   1.6502   +0.02    +3.79   1.5902\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab      1.5845   1.5865   -0.13    -2.05   1.6176\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab      1.4482   1.4484   -0.01    +0.19   1.4455\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab      0.6054   0.6050   +0.07    -7.76   0.6563\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab   1.6070   1.6068   +0.01    +0.21   1.6036\par  Euro/stg\tab \tab     0.6133   0.6137   -0.07    -1.70   0.6239.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw32063bt\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Treasury and Federal Reserve foriegn exchange  \highlight22\uc2 operations \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  Deborah L Leonard \par \uc2 2,410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par \uc2  Federal Reserve Bulletin \par \uc2 FRB\par  206-211 \par \uc2  Volume 86, Issue 3; ISSN: 0014-9209 \par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Bell & Howell Information and Learning Company. All rights reserved. Copyright Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Mar 2000 \par \par \uc2  This report, presented by Peter R. Fisher, Executive Vice President, Federal Reserve Bank of New York, and Manager, System Open Market Account, describes the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the US. Department of the Treasury and the Federal Reserve System for the period from October through December 1999. Deborah L. Leonard was primarily responsible for preparation of the report. \par \par \uc2  During the fourth quarter of 1999, the dollar depreciated 3.7 percent against the yen and appreciated 6.2 percent against the euro. On an effective tradeweighted basis, the dollar appreciated 0.8 percent. Market expectations for sustained economic growth in the United States provided underlying support for the dollar and U.S. equity markets. Changes in the dollar's value were also influenced by portfolio flows that contributed to the continued appreciation of the yen and depreciation of the euro. Daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading volumes fell sharply toward the very end of the quarter, although financial markets demonstrated no discernible signs of dislocation before the century date change. The U.S. monetary authorities did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during the quarter. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOVEMENTS DOMINATED BY YEN STRENGTH AND EURO WEAKNESS \par \par \uc2  Over the course of the fourth quarter, the yen rose steadily to a four-year high of Y101.64 against the dollar and an all-time high of 102.60 against the euro. The yen's appreciation accompanied continued perceptions of economic improvement in Japan, capital flows into Japanese assets, and uncertainty regarding the prospects for additional stimulus by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The movement prompted several publicly confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  Market participants cited modest signs of improvement in several key Japanese economic data reports and revisions to previous reports as evidence that Japan's economic cycle was turning. Tankan business sentiment indexes in September and December of -22 and -17, respectively, reached the survey's highest levels in two years, although the improvement mainly reflected the results for large manufacturing firms. On November 15, Japan's third-quarter industrial production was reported to have risen 3.9 percent since the previous quarter. On December 6, Japan's third-quarter gross domestic product was reported to have contracted 1.0 percent for the quarter; second-quarter GDP growth, however, was revised upward from 0.1 to 1.0 percent. Market participants also noted three consecutive positive reports of the Economic Planning Agency's monthly leading and coincident diffusion indexes. The Japanese government's November 11 announcement of an Y18 trillion supplementary spending package, including an expected Y6.5 trillion of actual spending, was perceived as demonstrating an ongoing government commitment to employing fiscal stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  At the outset of the quarter, attention focused on the prospects for the Bank of Japan to adopt "quantitative easing" measures to invigorate Japan's economy further. The Bank of Japan's announcements of measures aimed at improving the flexibility of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and at providing ample liquidity over the turn of the year were perceived as introducing technical changes that did not alter the stance of Japanese monetary policy. Short-term interest rates in Japan rose marginally over the quarter, at times affected by continued evidence of improving economic conditions, strength in the equity markets, and comments from Bank of Japan officials regarding the extraordinary nature of the zero interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  Expressing growing confidence in prospects for an upswing in Japan's economy, foreign investors increased their direct and portfolio capital investments in Japan. The Nikkei rose to a two-year high while broader Japanese equity indexes rose even more sharply, buoyed by evidence of corporate restructuring and the global rally in the equity markets. As foreign portfolio inflows continued, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that foreign direct investment rose to Y1.3 trillion during the first half of 1999, more than twice the amount during the same period in 1998. In the meantime, the yen's persistent appreciation continued to reduce the value of unhedged foreign assets for Japanese investors, further encouraging hedging or liquidation of those positions. Some Japanese investors who were reluctant to maintain large overseas exposures amid year-end uncertainty, reportedly repatriated capital or curtailed investment outflows before the century date change, thus creating additional demand for yen. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, perceptions that the euro-area economic outlook had improved, views that there were upside risks of inflation, and commentary from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) officials prompted heightened expectations of an interest rate hike by the ECB during the fourth quarter. Expectations solidified after October 27, when year-on-year M3 money supply in the euro area was reported to have grown 6.1 percent in September, outpacing the ECB's target rate of 4.5 percent. On November 4, the ECB raised its two-week refinancing rate 50 basis points, to 3.00 percent. Commenting after the action, ECB President Duisenberg said that the "timely rise [in interest rates] will avoid the need for a bigger rise later." After the move, the yield implied by the March 2000 Euribor futures contract fell to its period low of 3.46 percent, and European sovereign bond yields fell, as market participants noted that the rate rise helped to mitigate expectations of a sustained cycle of monetary tightening in Europe. Yields rebounded in the second half of the quarter, with ten-year sovereign benchmark bond yields rising approximately 50 basis points but remaining below their period highs, amid global declines in bond prices and as economic data pointed to stronger European growth. Germany's 3.2 percent rise in October manufacturing orders, 1.7 percent rise in industrial production the same month, and a better-thanexpected November business climate survey by the German IFO Institute all supported the improved expectations for growth. Annualized third-quarter growth in the euro area of 2.3 percent also exceeded consensus forecasts. \par \par \uc2  Despite higher domestic interest rates and economic growth prospects, the euro weakened steadily against the dollar and the yen. On December 2 and 3, the euro briefly dipped below $1.00. Throughout the quarter, the effect on the euro of widening U.S.European yield differentials and reported global portfolio reallocations appeared to outweigh Europe's improving growth outlook and robust gains in its equity markets. In the first half of the quarter, market participants noted sizable euro sales as portfolio managers-who reportedly had relatively high euro weightings relative to their benchmarks-moved toward more neutral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in early preparation for the year-end. Net speculative positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures exchanges, as well as survey flow data, appeared to show similar patterns. Japanese investors reportedly continued to hedge their European investments to protect against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses on capital investments. \par \par \uc2  Market participants also noted the resilience of U.S. asset markets and widening yield differentials as factors weighing on the euro. The annualized yield spread between the benchmark ten-year U.S. Treasury and German bund widened from 89 to 119 basis points by the end of the quarter. European sovereign bonds outperformed U.S. Treasury securities as market participants estimated that (1) inflation in Europe would remain relatively low, (2) that moderate European rate hikes in the future were already reflected in prices, and (3) that a more pronounced cycle of monetary tightening in the United States was under way. Lastly, some cited concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by European governments in mergers and acquisitions and bankruptcy proceedings could signal a slowing in corporate restructuring efforts in the euro area. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR SUPPORTED BY EXPECTATIONS FOR SUSTAINED U.S. GROWTH \par \par \uc2  Despite its mixed performance against the euro and the yen, the dollar's value remained relatively stable on a trade-weighted basis. When measured broadly against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major trading partners, the dollar appreciated 0.8 percent, while the yen appreciated 6.3 percent, and the euro depreciated 4.4 percent during the fourth quarter. The relative resilience of U.S. asset markets amid tightening monetary conditions in the United States provided underlying support for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Yields on U.S. Treasury securities rose to their highest levels in more than two years after the Federal Open Market Committee's (FOMC) adoption of a tightening bias at its October 5 meeting and its decision to raise the federal funds target rate from 5.25 to 5.50 percent on November 16. Data releases throughout the quarter were perceived to show strong, non-inflationary U.S. growth that contributed to a 20-basis-point flattening of the two-to-thirty-year Treasury coupon curve to a fifteen-month low of 23 basis points. While data continued to show somewhat modest upward pressures on earnings, producer prices, and consumer prices, stronger-than-expected growth, and consumption data raised market expectations that subsequent rate hikes would moderate the pace of U.S. growth. The third-quarter employment cost index, reported on October 28, fell from 1.1 percent in the second quarter to 0.8 percent in the fourth quarter. Employment reports released during the quarter generally showed lower-than-expected gains in nonfarm payrolls and average hourly earnings. However, third-quarter GDP growth of 4.8 percent and a deflator of 0.9 percent, reported on October 28, were revised upward to 5.5 percent and 1.1 percent, respectively, on November 24. September and October retail sales figures, excluding autos, were also higher than expected. Market participants perceived that the tone of the FOMC's December 21 directive was relatively hawkish, even though the Committee maintained its symmetric directive "in light of market uncertainties associated with the century date change." By the end of the quarter, the implied yield on the February federal funds futures contract had risen 37 basis points, to 5.79 percent. \par \par \uc2  Despite rising bond yields, U.S. and global equity markets surged to record highs as market participants expressed increased confidence in the prospects for sustained global growth and as global liquidity remained high. The Dow Jones Industrial Average and the S&P 500 indexes rose 11.2 and 14.5 percent, respectively, while the Nasdaq skyrocketed 48.2 percent. The momentum contributing to the Nasdaq's performance accelerated after the October 28 release of third-quarter GDP and employment cost figures, as well as Chairman Greenspan's speech on the same day noting the contributions that technology has made to U.S. productivity and recent economic performance. \par \par \uc2  Higher interest rates in the United States did not appear to weigh negatively on sentiment toward emerging-market assets. On the contrary, confidence in global growth prospects fueled investor interest in emerging-market assets throughout the quarter. Spreads between emerging-market sovereign debt and comparable U.S. Treasury securities narrowed, with the spread between J.P. Morgan's Emerging Market Bond Index (EMBI+) sovereign yield and U.S. Treasury securities having fallen 274 basis points, to 824 basis points-its lowest level since August 1998. Moreover, Latin American and Asian telecommunication stocks rallied along with other global technology shares, as growth in those regions continued to improve from technology exports and gains in productivity. \par \par \uc2  ORDERLY FINANCIAL MARKETS BEOFRE THE Y2K TURN \par \par \uc2  As the year's end approached, uncertainties associated with the century date change did not create any visible distress in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Overall, trading volumes reportedly remained within their average ranges for 1999 until well into Decemberlonger than many would have predicted earlier in the year. By December 31, however, trading volumes had ground nearly to a halt as market participants postponed all but absolutely necessary transactions until the new year. \par \par \uc2  Trading activity appeared orderly throughout the period, although bid-ask spreads on major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs more than doubled in the last trading days of the year. However, volatility implied by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options remained in recent ranges in the final weeks of the year as spot market activity diminished. Although some portfolio managers who were concerned about year-end liquidity reportedly moved toward more neutral positions relative to their benchmarks early in the period, other indications by the end of the quarter suggested that broadly based risk aversion was not prevalent in the financial markets. Global stock markets rallied amid relatively heavy trading volume through December, confidence in prospects for emerging-market growth prospects improved, and credit and swap spreads narrowed. \par \par \uc2  TREASURY AND FEDERAL RESERVE \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  RESERVES \par \par \uc2  The U.S. monetary authorities did not undertake any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the quarter. At the end of the quarter, the current values of euro and Japanese yen reserve holdings totaled $16.1 billion for the Federal Reserve System and $16.2 billion for the Exchange Stabilization Fund. The U.S. monetary authorities invest all of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  balances in a variety of instruments that yield marketrelated rates of return and have a high degree of liquidity and credit quality. To the greatest extent practicable, these investments are split evenly between the Federal Reserve System and the Exchange Stabilization Fund. \par \par \uc2  A portion of the balances is invested in German and Japanese government securities held directly or under repurchase agreement. Government securities held under repurchase agreement are arranged either through transactions executed directly in the market or through agreements with official institutions. Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are also invested in deposits at the Bank for International Settlements and facilities at other official institutions. As of December 31, direct holdings of foreign government securities totaled $9.2 billion, split evenly between the two authorities. Foreign government securities held under repurchase agreement totaled $13.7 billion at the end of the quarter and were also split evenly between the two authorities. \par \par \uc2 Dokument frb0000020010806dw310000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
