\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hits dollar parity in U.S. trading. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2000\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 25 (Reuters) - The euro fell below one to one with the dollar on Tuesday, slipping through the historic level for the second time in seven weeks as hopes for a rebound in U.S. stocks helped accelerate early morning selling. \par \par \uc2  The euro dipped as low as $0.9988, according to Reuters data, stopping just short of its life time low at $0.9986 set on Dec. 6. \par \par \uc2  The one-year old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen more than a full cent from the day's high of $1.0096 set in Asia on the back of Monday's 2.16 percent fall in the Dow Jones Industrials Average before bouncing back to trade near $1.0036. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is definitely offered and eventually it will drift lower still but there is a sense that someone is holding it up near the lower levels," said ING Barings senior vice president John McCarthy in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro started to descend in Europe with traders expecting the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to test parity for the second consecutive day as a 5.1 point rise in March S&P futures suggested a firmer opening for the Dow Jones industrial average. \par \par \uc2  But the Dow's lackluster start with a 14 point loss helped slow the euro's decline as trading rooms were abuzz with rumours that some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have lent a hand in preventing further losses. "Everyone is talking about some big player but we can't pin this on anyone," a New York based trader said. \par \par \uc2  The Dow later turned higher. \par \par \uc2  A heavy snowstorm sweeping up the Eastern coast of the United States caused the cancellation of Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony in Washington and left many New York dealing rooms thinly staffed early on as employees struggled to get to work. \par \par \uc2  "Since Greenspan is not going to speak today, there may well be a little relief rally on Wall Street and that could surely accelerate problems for the euro," another New York based trader said, adding that thinned trading conditions triggered by late arrivals and early departures could hasten further euro declines later. \par \par \uc2  Even though the Dow wilted on Monday, analysts remained generally confident the Federal Reserve will succeed in keeping inflation under control, allowing the unprecedented U.S. expansion to continue. Therefore Wall Street's potentially negative impact on the dollar is seen to be slim. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the euro also failed to draw much support from a batch of euro-zone data showing that euro-zone economic recovery was broadly on track. \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices in the German state of Hesse rose an annual 2.0 percent in January from 1.5 percent in December. In the state of North Rhine Westphalia CPI rose 1.9 percent from 1.4 percent in December, while Saxony's consumer prices rose 1.4 percent from 0.6 percent previously. \par \par \uc2  "There has been dramatic improvement in the euro zone data over the last six months. But the euro seems impervious to euro zone fundamentals. Until the Dow loses significantly or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials' comments change in tone, I don't think the euro is going to go anywhere," said Matthew Clements, economist at Prebon Marshall in London. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street was hit when Atlanta Fed President Jack Guynn, a voting Federal Open Market Committee member, said on Monday he did not see signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's three interest rate hikes in 1999 were slowing consumer demand. This reignited talk of an aggressive U.S. interest rate tightening. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the yen was mired in familiar ranges against the euro and the dollar after the Group of Seven rich industrialised nations at the weekend meeting said they shared Japan's concern over the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said in Tokyo that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address disruptive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, although no such movements had been seen so far on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  While the euro pulled back against the yen, it remained above Monday's three-week low at 104.80. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was pegged just near 105.69, about one yen above Monday's lows of 104.62. \par \par \uc2  ((U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212 859 1639)). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 9:51 a.m. (1451 GMT).  All data taken from \par \par \uc2  Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close \par \par \uc2  at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %    1999\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab Jan. 24\tab \tab \tab  Close\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    1.0047   1.0075   -0.28   -0.23   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     105.66   105.65   +0.01   +3.38   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    106.13   106.30   -0.16   +2.94   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.6044   1.5997   +0.29   +0.89   1.5902\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.6496   1.6510   -0.08   +1.98   1.6176\par Can/dlr\tab \tab     1.4416   1.4418   -0.01   -0.27   1.4455\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab     0.6550   0.6540   +0.15   -0.20   0.6563\par Euro/swiss\tab \tab  1.6125   1.6111   +0.09   +0.56   1.6036\par Euro/stg\tab \tab    0.6091   0.6094   -0.05   -2.37   0.6239\par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 f