\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Soggy euro slumps near dollar parity in U.S. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2000\par 22:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 25 (Reuters) - The euro fell on Tuesday, slipping below the key $1.00 level for the second time in seven weeks as Wall Street stocks moved into positive territory. \par \par \uc2  The year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as low as 99.88 cents, according to Reuters data, coming within a hair of its lifetime low of 99.86 cents set on Dec 6. \par \par \uc2  "We haven't really seen the end of euro weakness," said Susan Stearns, vice president, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Montreal, adding that 97.00 cents marks a key downside target. \par \par \uc2  Following the pattern set on Monday, the dollar tracked the movements of U.S. stocks, allowing the euro to take a short-lived shot at a comeback near midday as shares slumped. \par \par \uc2  But as the Dow Jones industrial average zigzagged back into positive territory, Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated near its lows for the day just above the key $1.00 level, down more than half a percent from Monday's closing level of $1.0075. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the morning's seesaw action came as little surprise. \par \par \uc2  "The correlation between the stock market and the euro is still intact," explained Rick Barnett, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Royal Bank of Canada in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  The euro had briefly found its footing in the U.S. session as hopes faded that the Dow would wipe away Monday's 2.16-percent fall. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is definitely offered, and eventually it will drift lower still, but there is a sense that someone is holding it up near the lower levels," said ING Barings senior vice president John McCarthy in New York. \par \par \uc2  Trading rooms were abuzz with rumours some central banks may have lent a hand in preventing further euro losses, but there was little hard evidence of what stopped the euro from setting fresh life lows on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "There was talk of decent names buying euros at the lows, but we cannot confirm anything," Barnett said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the near-term outlook remains gloomy for the euro, which has lost about 15 percent of its value against the dollar since its debut one year ago, plagued by uncertainty over the euro zone's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  A heavy snowstorm sweeping up the U.S. East Coast was blamed for some of the erratic movements as many New York trading floors were thinly staffed. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, the Senate Budget Committee cancelled Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony, robbing markets of the day's main focus. \par \par \uc2  Despite the Dow's erratic behaviour, analysts remained generally confident the Federal Reserve will succeed in keeping inflation under control, allowing the unprecedented U.S. expansion to continue. Therefore, Wall Street's potentially negative impact on the dollar is seen to be slim. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the euro also failed to draw much support from a batch of euro-zone data showing that euro-zone economic recovery was on broadly on track. \par \par \uc2  "There has been dramatic improvement in the euro zone data over the last six months. But the euro seems impervious to euro zone fundamentals. Until the Dow loses significantly or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials' comments change in tone, I don't think the euro is going to go anywhere," said Matthew Clements, economist at Prebon Marshall in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the yen was mired in familiar ranges against the euro and the dollar after the Group of Seven rich industrial nations at the weekend meeting said they shared Japan's concern over the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said in Tokyo that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address disruptive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, although no such movements had been seen so far on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro slumped about a quarter of a percent against the yen to stand near 106, but traded comfortably above Monday's three-week low at 104.80. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen extended its gains to stand about one quarter of a percent higher just under 106 yen, more than one and a quarter yen above Monday's lows of 104.62. \par \par \uc2  ((U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212 859 1607)). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 2130GMT.  All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at \par \par \uc2  2130GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %  1999\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cls\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab 24 Jan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab    1.0004   1.0075   -0.70   -0.66   1.0070\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab     106.00   105.65   +0.33   +3.71   102.21\par Euro/yen\tab \tab    106.03   106.30   -0.25   +2.84   103.10\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab   1.6110   1.5997   +0.71   +1.31   1.5902\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab     1.6440   1.6510   -0.42   +1.63   1.6176\par Can/dlr\tab \tab     1.4362   1.4418   -0.39   -0.64   1.4455\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab     0.6550   0.6540   +0.15   -0.20   0.6563\par Euro/swiss\tab \tab  1.6123   1.6111   +0.07   +0.54   1.6036\par Euro/stg\tab \tab    0.6082   0.6094   -0.20   -2.52   0.6239\par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28