\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dlr May See Post-G7 Dip, But Support Solid \b0\par\par\uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2000\par 20:59\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  (This story originally moved on Saturday.) \par \par \uc2  By Arran Scott \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--There were no surprises in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at Saturday's meeting of top finance officials from the Group of Seven nations - and for that reason the dollar may dip initially against the yen when trading gets underway Monday. \par \par \uc2  Last week's market activity ended with a round of dollar selling against the yen as traders squared positions, increasingly convinced that the G7 wouldn't deliver a strong endorsement of Japan's efforts to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The G7 did indeed fail to deliver that endorsement - which means this week may begin where last week left off, with one last round of dollar long liquidation, dealers said. However, after its initial dip, the dollar will probably find solid support around the mid-Y103 level, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There might be dollar selling at first, but the downside will be limited. I don't think people will sell agressively, because the G7 turned out just as we expected," said Shuji Takano, assistant manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ABN AMRO Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  After their meeting in Tokyo, the G7 finance ministers released a statement that read in part: "We welcome the reaffirmation by the Japanese monetary authorities of their intention to conduct policies appropriately in view of their concerns, which we share, about the potential impact of yen appreciation for the Japanese economy and the world economy." \par \par \uc2  The wording didn't break any new ground, and was essentially the same as a statement which G7 ministers made about the yen last September. The message from the G7 was essentially that it's up to Tokyo to see to it that the yen doesn't rise further; hence the emphasis on Tokyo's "intention to conduct policies appropriately". Officials who attended the meeting said there was no discussion of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or of specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. Although U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers reiterated that "a strong dollar is in the interest of the United States," he stopped short of endorsing a weaker yen or of claiming that the dollar is currently too weak. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is that the G7 isn't going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly," said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But even without the G7's help, the yen may struggle to rise much further against the dollar, traders said. For one thing, many believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively - as it has at intervals during recent months - if the yen rises sharply on Monday. And interest rates also appear to be on the side of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  G7 officials agreed that despite signs of recovery in Japan's economy, it remains on a shaky footing and Tokyo must continue to provide it with fiscal and monetary support. In response, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami assured the meeting that the BOJ isn't about to abandon its zero interest rate policy any time soon. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, rates are expected to continue rising in the U.S. This could cause traders to shift their focus from the Japanese recovery to diverging interest rate differentials between Europe, Japan and the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "Interest rates will be the focus after the G7," Takano said. "There are bulls and bears, but I'm on the bull side in the dollar/yen and euro/yen because the next focus is absolute interest rate diffentials between the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a 0.25 percentage point interest rate hike at the Feb. 2 meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve Open Market Committee is already factored into the market, but many believe further U.S. hikes are possible later this year. As a result, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will closely watch Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's Senate testimony on Tuesday and Wednesday in the hope of finding clues to the Fed's intentions. \par \par \uc2  In the meantime, initial support for dollar/yen is expected around Y104.00 with firm technical support pegged at Y103.60, dealers said; resistance for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lies at Y105.50 and again at Y106.60. The dollar traded at Y104.74 late in the New York session on Friday. \par \par \uc2  If the G7 didn't stick its neck out on the yen, it didn't do so on the euro either. Some traders had expected some reference to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the G7 statement, given the euro's continued weakness since its launch a little over a year ago, and its effect in fueling the U.S. trade deficit with Europe. \par \par \uc2  But the G7 didn't mention the euro at all in its statement - in fact, European officials emphasized after the meeting that there was no need to talk up the euro for now. French Finance Minister Christian Sautter told reporters that G7 officials had seen no reason to touch on the euro's level in the communique. \par \par \uc2  "The euro-zone fundamentals are good so there was no need to mention it," Sautter said. \par \par \uc2  Many traders therefore expect the euro to move little in the immediate aftermath of the G7 meeting, though some believe it could edge higher on the back of steady portfolio investment into the euro zone. Expectations of a 0.25 percentage point European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate hike by the end of March should provide support. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders forecast the euro will move between $1.0000 and $1.0350 during\par the week; it was trading at $1.0098 in New York late Friday.\par -By Arran Scott; (813) 5255-2929; arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010805dw1n000o9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
