\par\par \b \uc2  Economy, yen and IMF successor to top G7's agenda. \b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2000\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 21 (Reuters) - Following is a summary of the issues expected to dominate Saturday's meeting in Tokyo of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations plus Russia. \par \par \uc2  WORLD ECONOMY \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Friday that the outlook for the global economy was bright. But U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers says he wants both Japan and Europe to raise their growth sights by redoubling their commitment to boost domestic demand and push through deep-seated reforms. \par \par \uc2  This is because the United States is growing too fast for comfort, resulting in a huge trade deficit and an even bigger private-sector financial deficit. \par \par \uc2  Capital inflows, attracted by the soaring stock market and America's technology-driven productivity boom, have so far enabled America to live beyond its means. But policy-makers and markets fret that a Wall Street crash could bring the U.S. economy down to earth with a bump. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Tokyo is pressing hard for G7 help to prevent a further rise in the yen as this would hobble the export sector, one of the few sources of growth in Japan's still-fragile economy. The G7 will probably comply, but economists are sceptical that a simple statement would set the yen back. \par \par \uc2  Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's partners is considered a remote possibility, at least as long as the dollar remains above Japan's pain threshold of 100 yen. On Friday it stood around 105. \par \par \uc2  Washington would like the G7 to call for a recovery in the bedraggled euro. European officials, confident the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will recover on its own, oppose drawing attention to its weakness. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND \par \par \uc2  Ministers are sure to discuss a successor to IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus, who steps down next month. The job is traditionally held by a European and Germany is lobbying hard for its deputy finance minister, Caio Koch-Weser. \par \par \uc2  But France and the United States are luke-warm in their support, vastly reducing Koch-Weser's chances. Andrew Crockett, a Briton who runs the Bank for International Settlements, and Italian Treasury Director Mario Draghi want the job, but are probably too technocratic for a post that also demands a deft political touch. Camdessus's deputy, Stanley Fischer, could be asked to take the helm in the interim. \par \par \uc2  EMERGING ECONOMIES \par \par \uc2  The G7 can breathe more easily now that Asia especially is recovering strongly from its financial crisis. But ministers, worried that complacency might set in, are likely to urge governments to seize the opportunity of solid growth to push through corporate and financial restructuring and other reforms. \par \par \uc2  RUSSIA \par \par \uc2  The G7 will be looking for reassurances from Russian First Deputy Prime Minister Mikhail Kasyanov, who will attend part of Saturday's talks, that Moscow will plough ahead with economic reforms under acting President Vladimir Putin. The fate of a delayed $640 million IMF loan instalment to Moscow will also be on the agenda, with sections of international opinion opposed to a quick disbursement because of the rising civilian death toll in Moscow's war against Islamic rebels in Chechnya. \par \par \uc2  Ukraine might also be discussed. Cash-strapped Kiev is seeking to restructure its $2 billion in foreign debt to unfreeze a $2.6 billion IMF aid package. \par \par \uc2  OTHER ISSUES \par \par \uc2  - Ministers will take stock of the progress of a plan agreed last June to strengthen the global financial system to avert a repeat of crises in 1997 and 1998 that shook world markets. \par \par \uc2  - They are also expected to review progress of an ambitious plan agreed at last June's Cologne summit to expand debt relief for 41 heavily indebted poor countries. \par \par \uc2  - Legal questions raised by the boom in global electronic commerce will also be on the agenda, according to the Yomiuri Shimbun, a Japanese daily. ((Alan Wheatley, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8453 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw1l0216h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
