\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen buoyant, scepticism abounds on G7 concern. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2000\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 21 (Reuters) - The yen strengthened across the board on Friday as scepticism grew whether the Group of Seven financial superpowers would strongly endorse a weaker yen when they meet in Tokyo on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Finance Ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of the seven nations were seen unlikely to step up their rhetoric on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and there was growing doubt if they would even repeat the wording they used at their last meeting in September - that they shared Japan's concern about the yen's export crippling strength. \par \par \uc2  "It's looking obvious to the market that what will come out of the G7 would probably be nothing more than a restatement of what was out in September," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "Even if we were to get something surprisingly aggressive from the G7, what does it mean?" he questioned. "The G7 has 'shared' Japan's concern about the yen since September, but no other central banker (besides the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has dipped into his pocket to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Such views were reinforced by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Economist Otmar Issing's comment that the ECB had no stance on the yen and German Finance Minister Hans Eichel's remark that it made no sense to act against the market trend. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said in Tokyo that the United States and Japan shared concern over imbalances in the global economy, but urged Japan to focus on boosting domestic demand and restructuring. \par \par \uc2  "We think the U.S. is still reluctant to be involved in a move toward joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We saw another record trade deficit yesterday, and engineering a weaker yen is only going to exacerbate that," said Mike Moran, treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose around one percent on the day against both the dollar and the euro. Dollar/yen fell to two-week lows below 104.60, while euro/yen fell below 106. \par \par \uc2  Yen buying was initially triggered after Tokyo's Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said a stronger yen was not necessarily detrimental for the Japanese economy. Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he did not know what the G7 would conclude regarding the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The markets have taken the comments from Japanese officials to mean that the G7 would not mention the yen at all," said Neil Parker, treasury economist at the Royal Bank of Scotland. "There had been hopes in some quarters but those have been pushed to the side." \par \par \uc2  DOW HURTS DOLLAR, EURO WOBBLY \par \par \uc2  A 1.2-percent drop in the Dow Jones industrial average on Thursday also dampened sentiment on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We're coming to a critical moment where U.S. equities are starting to look a bit more vulnerable," said Standard Chartered's Moran. "They really have been the compensating force which has been able to let the U.S. build up such a large (current account) deficit." \par \par \uc2  Although the euro rose to one-week highs against the dollar near $1.02 on Thursday, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed gains amid a lack of follow-through buying, failing to capitalise on supportive comments by European monetary officials. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar and euro took turns taking a beating, sterling shined. The pound stuck close to Thursday's record high of around 61 pence against the euro and tested three-month peaks against the dollar above $1.6550. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was well supported as expectations of higher UK interest rates had been boosted by the string of strong economic reports this week. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw1l020ig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
