\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW - Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2000\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  On the issue of what the forthcoming Group of Seven meeting is likely to say about the yen, currently a key focus of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Medley told Reuters he expects the G7 to mention the yen again in a statement on Saturday, perhaps reiterating the participants' shared concern over the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  Such concern was expressed in a joint statement following the last meeting of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs in September. \par \par \uc2  But Medley added, "I haven't heard anything that would lead me to think they'll go beyond that." \par \par \uc2  Medley, former adviser to financier George Soros and head of the New York firm Medley Global Advisors, has been in Tokyo this week meeting officials from Japan and other G7 countries ahead of Saturday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets have been on tenterhooks wondering whether the G7 will offer Japan verbal support in its efforts to combat the yen's strength and what promises Tokyo might have to make in return. \par \par \uc2  Medley said that Tokyo would agree, under U.S. pressure, to promise to maintain its fiscal stimulus and for the Bank of Japan at least to continue its policy of pushing short-term interest rates virtually to zero. \par \par \uc2  The United States would like to see the BOJ go further in easing credit, such as expanding its money market fund surplus to weaken the yen, but a pledge from BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami to continue the zero interest rate policy "is as far as anyone's going to get", he said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has left a one trillion yen daily surplus in the money market since last May, but pumped that up to some 20 trillion yen over the New Year to cushion the impact of any Y2K-related fund demand. This week, though, it drained the market surplus back to about one trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  Medley said the United States believes that Y2K-related excess liquidity buttressed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's January 4 yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "They're saying, 'If you (BOJ) want the yen to weaken, it's in your hands'," he said. \par \par \uc2  But despite the market attention to a possible Japan-U.S. confrontation, Medley said he expected Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers to spend more time at the meeting "lecturing" the Europeans than the Japanese. \par \par \uc2  The United States is concerned about progress on European structural reform, the euro's continued weakness despite an economic upturn and the burgeoning scandal engulfing Germany's Christian Democratic Union, he said. \par \par \uc2  Summers will argue that Japan and Europe are "aiming too low on growth". He added the United States believes Japan should do more to close the gap between between oversupply and weak demand in the economy. \par \par \uc2  On U.S. policy, Medley said he expects the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates gradually by a total of at least 100 basis points this year, to slow the economy back to sustainable growth and extinguish isolated bubbles in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  ((William Mallard/Deborah Collis Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw1l01zyu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
