\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-MOF Gyohten: Hard To Muster G7 Yen Concern -Mainichi \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2000\par 02:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Toyoo Gyohten, a former senior official at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that Japan always faces a struggle in getting its Group of Seven partners to include phrases about strong yen concerns in statements released after their meetings, reported the Mainichi Shimbun Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with the newspaper, the former vice finance minister for international affairs said, "It's always difficult for Japan (to get the G7 to share concern over the strong yen)." \par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and central bankers from the G7 nations are scheduled to meet in Tokyo this Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Gyohten also said that Japan is at a disadvantage. Although the dollar-yen has a large effect on Japan's economy, it doesn't have such a big effect on the U.S. and European economies, he said. \par \par \uc2  Regarding a possible fall in the dollar below the Y100 level, Gyohten said he was unsure whether the other G7 nations would believe the dollar's plunge below the Y100 level is excessive until this actually happens, the newspaper reported. \par \par \uc2  However, Gyohten said that none of the other G7 partners would oppose coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there are sharp and excessive moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Other contributing factors for joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par are the elections in the U.S. and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year," Gyhoten said.\par    While Gyhoten said that the U.S. government is not worried about the\par dollar-yen, he said that he thinks the U.S. government doesn't want to\par see an outflow of capital out of U.S. stock markets as a result of a\par plunge in the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The U.S. stock market is in a bubble state, and the U.S. current\par account deficit is extremely large. Therefore, if the dollar falls, this\par could adversely affect stock prices. I don't think the U.S. side wants\par this," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In this weekend's G7 meeting, Gyohten said he expects the U.S. stock\par market bubble to be the main topic, the newspaper reported.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think there will also be discussions on economic recovery in Japan\par and East Asian countries and economic progress in the euro-zone and in\par Central and South America," Gyhoten said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gyohten is currently president of the Institute for International\par Monetary Affairs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw1k00ed4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
