\par\par \b \uc2  G7 Talks Billed As Routine; \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Said To Be Low Priority \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Pearson \par \uc2 908 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2000\par 19:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  PARIS -(Dow Jones)- What a difference a year makes. \par \par \uc2  Just 12 months ago, the global economy was licking its wounds from the Asian financial crisis that embroiled a whole region in 1997 and wounded dozens of countries by ricochet. \par \par \uc2  But when top monetary officials from the world's richest countries get together in Tokyo on Jan. 22, they'll be looking at a completely different picture. \par \par \uc2  "As G7 meetings go, this one isn't likely to stick out in people's minds," according to one official involved in its preparation. \par \par \uc2  It will be a regular "surveillance" meeting at which finance ministers and central bankers take stock of the world economy and financial markets and review the work being done to strengthen the international financial system. \par \par \uc2  This routine session - the G7 get together at least three times a year - takes place against a backdrop of a U.S. economy that's still puffing along nicely while Europe is looking more robust with every day that passes. And in the emerging countries, the scars of the Asian financial crisis are healing faster than expected. \par \par \uc2  The main clouds on the horizon these days are Japan's rickety economy and bloated equity prices in the U.S. and elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  Japanese finance ministry officials not withstanding, the level of the yen isn't likely to be a major topic. On Wednesday, the yen was trading at nearly Y106.00 to the dollar, compared with around Y103.75 just before the last G7 session in Washington on Sept. 25. \par \par \uc2  At that meeting, the G7 issued a 10-page statement saying they "shared Japan's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation for the Japanese economy" and pledged that they would "continue to monitor developments in exchange markets and cooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  What has changed in Japan since then to change the outlook for the yen? Admittedly, the economy has a bit more color in its cheeks, thanks to the pro-growth stance of fiscal and monetary policy, but the looming debt overhang and the reluctance of Japanese consumers to spend are still worrying. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think we'll hear anything different (from Sept. 25) from the Japanese when we go round the table," said a G7 ministerial aide. "You know, there's not much the G6 can do about the situation there. The Japanese have to figure it out for themselves." \par \par \uc2  The Jan. 22 statement, therefore, could reaffirm the September meeting's language as regards G7 concern about the yen, so long as the Japanese reaffirm publicly they will keep fiscal and monetary policies stimulative. But don't look for any strong language by the G7 on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to correct the yen. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Wednesday, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami disclosed that he's going to tell his G7 colleagues that the BOJ's zero interest rate policy will be pursued. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is likely to stress to his European counterparts that, while activity is picking up strongly in Europe - largely thanks to low interest rates and a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - more reforms are needed to promote sustained growth. Your heart may be beating faster, he may say, but your arteries are still clogged. \par \par \uc2  The Europeans and the Japanese, in turn, may voice their continuing concern over the size of the U.S. balance of payments gap. \par \par \uc2  But there's one area where all the G7 partners share a common fear: that the asset price bubbles in the U.S. and elsewhere will burst suddenly. That would really spoil the party. \par \par \uc2  The world's central bankers - starting with the U.S. Federal Reserve's Alan Greenspan - are walking on eggs these days. \par \par \uc2  With inflation edging up in Europe and in the U.S., their normal reflex would be to tighten monetary policy. But the central bankers are now hostage to the equity markets and scared that raising interest rates may spark an equity market selloff. \par \par \uc2  If a stock market crash did materialize, of course, they would have to throw the machine into reverse and pump liquidity into the system. \par \par \uc2  International economists say the big question isn't so much if there will be a market correction, or even when, but how. Will the dollar peel off before the NYSE or vice-versa? The G7 ministers and central bankers will no doubt be presented with different scenarios by IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus. \par \par \uc2  It's not on the official agenda, but officials say they would be surprised if there isn't some discussion about who should replace Camdessus when he steps down in mid-February. That's a topic that's more likely to be aired during the informal dinners that usually follow these events, however. \par \par \uc2  Russia's failure to meet targets it had agreed with the IMF is likely to be raised when Russian Finance Minister and First Deputy Prime Minister Mikhail Kasyanov and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman Viktor Gerashchenko join the Saturday afternoon discussions. \par \par \uc2  And the failed World Trade Organization ministerial meeting last month in Seattle should also be touched upon. \par \par \uc2  Finally, the G7 will talk about strengthening the international financial system, but officials say it would be wrong to expect any decisions out of the Tokyo meeting, as the forward momentum in this area has slowed noticeably. \par \par \uc2  -By David Pearson; +33 1 40171740; david.pearson@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1108 4207 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010805dw1j00abd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
