\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Japan bemoans strong yen ahead of G7 meet. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2000\par 10:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 18 (Reuters) - Japan lamented the yen's strength as expectations faded on Tuesday that its Group of Seven partners will offer even verbal support this weekend for reining in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Top Japanese finance officials reiterated their concern that the high yen could derail a still-fragile recovery in the world's second-biggest economy, but shied away from openly calling for G7 finance ministers and central bankers to express such worries when they meet in Tokyo on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbed nearly 20 yen against the dollar as foreign investors rushed to buy Japanese stocks and other assets after the economy began to show last June that it was pulling out of its worst postwar downturn. \par \par \uc2  At the last G7 meeting in late September, the finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs said they "shared Japan's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation for the Japanese economy and the world economy". \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government fears its economy, recovering thanks to massive fiscal spending and near-zero interest rates, is not yet sturdy enough to withstand another spike in the yen's value. That could inflame deflationary pressure and erode the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings. \par \par \uc2  MOF APPEARS RESIGNED TO LONE FIGHT ON YEN \par \par \uc2  "The rapid strengthening of the yen since mid-last year has been out of line with such economic conditions," Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  czar, Haruhiko Kuroda, told Reuters, referring to the yen's 13 percent climb since then. \par \par \uc2  But asked whether Japan was pushing its G7 partners - the United States, Germany, Britain, France and Italy - to restate their shared concern over the strong yen, he said: "I can't say anything... until we meet". \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's boss, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, also indicated that Tokyo alone may be fretting over the strong yen, and reiterated Tokyo's determination to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the yen when it sees fit. \par \par \uc2  "When it is necessary, we need to respond. I want to say this clearly to the G7 this weekend," Miyazawa said in a speech on Tuesday, after explaining why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on January 4. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa noted that he had instructed his ministry to sell the yen if the dollar looked set to fall below 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  MARKETS SEE LITTLE HELP FROM U.S. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar recently stable around 105 yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said on Tuesday that doubts are growing whether the G7 will issue a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement or even reiterate their yen concerns. \par \par \uc2  The United States, in particular, has stressed that it is up to Japan to keep the fiscal and monetary spigots open until recovery is fully entrenched. \par \par \uc2  "We have urged publicly that Japan use all available means, fiscal and monetary, to continue the momentum of recovery until recovery is achieved," U.S. Ambassador Thomas Foley told reporters on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Washington is campaigning hard for Japan and Europe to do more for global growth, warning that the United States cannot keep carrying the burden alone. \par \par \uc2  BOJ SEEN ON HOLD \par \par \uc2  But Foley declined to prescribe specific policies for the Bank of Japan. A recent Japanese media report said Washington wanted the BOJ to confirm its willingness to ease its already hyper-loose monetary policy in return for cooperation on weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda declined to comment on whether Washington had exerted such pressure, but said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no choice but to maintain its zero interest rate policy for the time being. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ for nearly a year has said it will continue flooding the money market with liquidity to keep overnight rates virtually at zero until deflation fears abate. \par \par \uc2  ((With reporting by Tatsuo Ito. Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw1i04ozn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
