\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT: \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dlr Steady Ahead Of G7 Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2000\par 17:31\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  by Paul Evans \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- The prospect of rising interest rates across the world's leading industrialized economies and the continued strength of the Japanese yen will be key issues for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week, as finance ministers from the Group of Seven nations gather to meet Saturday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The week should begin on a quiet note as most U.S. markets close Monday for Martin Luther King Jr. Day and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers expect little change for the dollar against both the yen and the euro. \par \par \uc2  Talk of coordinated action by the world's top central banks to weaken the yen has weighed on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it flirted with 100 yen to $1 in late December. \par \par \uc2  But most analysts believe the Tokyo G7 summit will offer little beyond the kind of joint communique issued at the September 1999 G7 meeting in Washington, in which finance ministers groused about the strong yen and its potential effects on the Japanese and world economies, but ruled out joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of rhetoric about concerted G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Jeff Cheah, market strategist at Standard & Poor's MMS in Toronto. "But we haven't seen the G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a long time, and I don't think dollar/yen has got to a level where it's considered excessive enough to warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  That may prove "somewhat disappointing" to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said Lisa Finstrom, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Salomon Smith Barney in New York, but in her view shouldn't derail the dollar from grinding slowly stronger to about Y107 to Y108 this week as anticipation mounts ahead of the G-7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  But Cheah of Standard & Poor's MMS said that after the dollar spent last week dancing on both sides of the 106 yen mark, it's momentum may be exhausted for the time being, as Japanese exporters have continued to sell dollars on every spike higher. He said he expects the dollar to settle slightly lower between 104 and 105 yen this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's performance against the euro is expected to be strongly influenced by the outlook for European interest rates, as investors now look for clearer signs of sustained growth in euro-zone nations following the Bank of England's quarter-percentage point hike of its repo rate last week. \par \par \uc2  The euro's brief January rally was cut short last week as players signaled their disappointment with weak German November industrial production data Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar then edged up further against the euro and other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last week as U.S. asset markets enthused over soft U.S. December inflation data and statements by Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan that seemed to indicate the Fed is favoring only a gradual pace of monetary tightening in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Although it's regarded as too soon to expect higher rates from the euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Thursday's meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will draw some attention, as will the German IFO survey of business sentiment the same day. \par \par \uc2  "People continue to look for recovery out of Europe, but I'm not sure we'll be given any ammunition to allow the euro to advance this week," said Finstrom of Salomon Smith Barney, who expects to see the euro trading between $1.02 and $1.045. \par \par \uc2  "It looks pretty well-supported at these levels, just uninspired," she said. \par \par \uc2  Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in for more attention recently are the commodity-linked Canadian and Australian dollars. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers say that as expectations of rising interest rates and sustained growth in these economies take hold, the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are growing attractive as yield plays in addition to their traditional role as commodity plays. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar surged to its highest levels since early October last week on the back of a strong Australian December employment report, enhancing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players' expectations that the Reserve Bank of Australia will raise rates at its February 1 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar's rise last week was similarly keyed off expectations that Canada's strong economy will allow the Bank of Canada to raise interest rates at the same pace as in the U.S. Rising prices for crude oil and other raw materials also helped boost the commodity-sensitive Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  "There have been different stories for the commodity-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they're all ending up on the same road and making a stronger showing across the board," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. \par \par \uc2  Mooney and others said thin liquidity early in the week could provide the Canadian dollar with conditions equal to its late 1999 rally and allow it to challenge its year-to-date highs in the C$1.4400 area. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the European Union's decision to raise the drachma's central parity in the European exchange rate mechanism is expected to help Greece control its stubborn inflation and seal its entry in the European economic and monetary union in 2001. E.U. deputy finance ministers met in Brussels Saturday to discuss Greece's request to revalue the drachma's central parity in the European exchange rate mechanism. \par \par \uc2  The drachma's central parity was revalued Saturday to GRD340.75 to the euro, a 3.5% rise versus its previous ERM II parity of GRD353.109 to the euro. The drachma will keep trading within the broad 15% band in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  grid. \par \par \uc2  The E.U.'s economic and financial committee said in a statement that the decision will be accompanied "by continuing sound budgetary policies and the continuous pursuit of structural reforms" to strengthen the convergence process. \par \par \uc2  By Paul Evans, Dow Jones Newswires, (416) 306-2022 \par \par \uc2  paulr.evans@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Jenny Paris in Athens contributed to this article) \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010805dw1h000k8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
