\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-MOF Nakahira: Joint  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Always Possible \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hidenobu Kageyama \par \uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2000\par 07:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Joint yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always a possibility if the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates too sharply, a former senior official of Japan's Finance Ministry said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "It's always possible that (the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if undesirable market moves occur," said Kosuke Nakahira, who is currently vice-chairman of the Institute for International Economic Studies, in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  "A major risk to the Japanese economy is a rapid strengthening of the yen. If \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is properly dealt with, the economy might recover rapidly. Stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is indispensable for Japan," he added. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira was formerly a vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  He said that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be extensively discussed at the Tokyo meeting of the Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors on Jan. 22. \par \par \uc2  Stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are important not only for Japan, which is likely to push to make \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  a core topic at the G7 meeting, but also for the U.S. and European nations, Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  For the U.S., it's vital that exchange rates stay stable because that would help the nation's high-flying economy avoid a hard-landing, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed for the U.S. to avoid a hard-landing. For Europe, now is the time to build up confidence in the euro. European leaders don't want to see the euro drop below $1.0000 again. Stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a mutual desire among the G7 countries." \par \par \uc2  "There is no doubt that G7 nations will continue their coordination to stabilize moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. and Japanese economies will also figure prominently in the G7's discussions later this month, Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 will discuss how it can contribute to expanding the global economy. A risk of a hard landing of the U.S. economy, especially a possibility of a considerable drop in the U.S. stock market, will be discussed at the meeting," he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese economy will also be carefully assessed at the Tokyo\par meeting because there is concern among the G7 nations that Japan will\par have a hard time trying to get its economy back on an autonomous\par recovery track.\par -By Hidenobu Kageyama; 813-5255-2929; hidenobu.kageyama@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw1d005lf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
