\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : USD/JPY Down Amid Doubts G7 Wants Weaker Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2000\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar is lower against the yen in Asia Monday amid doubts the Group of Seven industrial nations would agree to any Japanese push for coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied sharply in European and New York trading Friday on reports Japan will seek support from other group members for a weaker yen. But an absence of comments on this matter by Japanese or other officials after a Tokyo meeting of G7 deputy finance ministers over the weekend has led most market participants to discount the likelihood of a coordinated approach. \par \par \uc2  "I think we would have heard some whispers coming out of Japan, or in the Japanese press if the G7 had been at all supportive of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  action," said a U.S. bank trader in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  G7 finance ministers are scheduled to meet Jan. 22 in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  With Japanese markets closed for a holiday Monday, trade in the U.S. dollar against the yen is thin. \par \par \uc2  Although the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could use the illiquid market conditions to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, most market participants expect the BOJ to wait until local markets reopen Tuesday before deciding whether to step into the market. \par \par \uc2  The rally Friday on Wall Street also reduces the likelihood of a dollar slide against the yen, and therefore removes a catalyst for BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders report dollar-buying interest at Y104.80 and offers at Y105.30. \par \par \uc2  "The bias is definitely toward dollar weakness as Japanese exporters will be keen sellers at these levels when Tokyo markets reopen," said a Singapore-based trader, also noting the presence of stop-loss sell orders below Y104.70. \par \par \uc2  Many Japanese companies have budgeted for a dollar around Y105 for the December-March period, which means they will be comfortable sellers at current levels to hedge their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (12:15 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y105.05, down from Y105.33 late Friday in New York and from Y105.35 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0295, barely changed from $1.0293 late Friday in New York and down from $1.0307 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y108.13, down from Y108.42 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y108.58 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6390, unchanged from late Friday in New York and down from $1.6475 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Trade in the euro is also quiet in Asia due to the Japanese holiday, with market participants reporting bids at US$1.0280 and offers at US$1.0300. \par \par \uc2  Inspiration for traders may come from a Bank for International Settlements meeting in Singapore of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from around the world. \par \par \uc2  Although \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of officials from institutions including the Bank of England, Bank of Japan, and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven't scheduled formal news conferences, they may comment on recent market developments on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley; 65 421 4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par              Tokyo Late      New York Late     Tokyo Late\par              Monday          Friday         