\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar firmer after robust U.S. data. \b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2000\par 16:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The dollar zig-zagged in early New York trading on Friday as dealers digested a fresh batch of strong U.S. economic numbers released at the tail end of a week characterised by very sharp moves. \par \par \uc2  The greenback briefly gained about half a cent against the euro, trimming some of this week's hefty losses after the U.S. Labour Department said payrolls added 315,000 jobs in December outside the farm sector. \par \par \uc2  This topped November's gain of 222,000 jobs and Wall Street's average estimate that some 224,000 jobs would be added in December. Hourly earnings rose 0.4 percent in December, topping expectations for a 0.3-percent rise. \par \par \uc2  The euro dropped as low as $1.0255 in early trading before curbing its losses to stand at $1.0299. \par \par \uc2  Despite the robust jobs numbers, dealers who had been expecting even worse inflation news scrambled to cover their short positions. Some dealers said a snappy recovery in U.S. blue chip stocks after sharp declines early in the week also helped buoy the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It is all a bit confusing, the market seems to be watching the Dow and there seems to be a bit of a recovery there, which has helped," said Rick Barnett, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Royal Bank of Canada in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  At 10:00 a.m. (1500 GMT), the Dow was up 107 points at 11,361. \par \par \uc2  "I would have expected more of a reaction, actually," said Henry Willmore, chief economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "We see a great deal of momentum in the economy and the risks of some pickup in inflation and the Fed having to tighten aggressively are certainly increasing with these kinds of numbers," Willmore added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the greenback climbed against the Japanese yen, rallying to a near-seven-week high after starting the session softer near 105. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has risen more than four percent against the yen this week to its 105.89 yen high as concerns about further Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weakness in Japanese equities weighed on the yen. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Tuesday to prop the dollar as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was probing four-year lows near 101 yen. It has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  some 14 times since June, fearing the yen's sharp rise will hinder Japan's nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  Traders were particularly nervous about possible action by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after comments by Japanese officials overnight underscored the BoJ's commitment to capping the yen. \par \par \uc2  Senior Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda said the yen's current level is excessively high and he hoped the correction of the yen's rise would continue. \par \par \uc2  Soon after, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if any rapid movements were detected and that a rise in the yen beyond 100 yen per dollar was undesirable. \par \par \uc2  The market was also keenly focused on a weekend meeting of Group of Seven industrialised nations, which lays the groundwork for a meeting of G7 finance minsters later this month. \par \par \uc2  "The fears are that at some point, if global markets continue to implode, the G7 may see some value in trying to shore up the dollar," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  an