\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen seen vulnerable to further weakness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2000\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The yen bobbed up from six-week lows against the dollar on Friday but was still fragile amid market speculation that Japan would find support for a weaker yen from its Group of Seven partners later this month. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen\tab   has retreated more than four percent against the dollar so far this week falling to around 105.60, its weakest since November 22 in Asian trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan managed to stave off yen gains earlier this week by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars on Tuesday and market concerns about further BOJ action were helping put a damper on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government sources said on Thursday G7 deputy finance ministers and central bankers would meet in Tokyo over the weekend in preparation for the meeting of finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs on January 22. \par \par \uc2  "The risk for dollar/yen to break below 100 is currently gone due to the hopes of the G7 meetings," said Michael Rottmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. \par \par \uc2  He said while no substantial message for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was likely after this weekend's meeting, the market was looking for a strong statement after the official meeting. \par \par \uc2  "We will probably get another statement within the communique that the G7 partners are still in favour of a somewhat weaker yen, or at least of a stable yen." \par \par \uc2  Japan's commitment to stemming the yen's appreciation was reconfirmed when Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said during Asian trading that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if any rapid movements were detected. \par \par \uc2  He added that he had told the ministry late last year to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar looked set to fall below 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  U.S. ASSETS REMAIN KEY FOCUS FOR DOLLAR \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts said the fortunes of U.S. asset markets were still the main concern for the dollar. The Dow Jones Industrials Average\tab   closed up 1.17 percent on Thursday.\par However, the Nasdaq composite index\tab    fell for the third successive day, a drop of nearly 10 percent from record highs hit on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. December payrolls data are due at 1330 GMT, and analysts said a strong report, particularly on the jobless rate and hourly earnings, would fuel speculation of an aggressive Federal Reserve tightening this year, which could well weigh on U.S. assets, and thus on the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The dollar - against the euro - will trade with the U.S. equity markets," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN AMRO in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Strong data are likely to be an intra-day negative for the dollar. Also, even if the employment report was to come out benign then the market is facing more uncertainty next week when both U.S. retail sales and CPI are due."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists surveyed by Reuters on average expected non-farm payrolls rise 224,000 in December, with the unemployment rate to stay at a low 4.1 percent and average hourly earnings to rise 0.3 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the euro\tab   was consolidating this week's gains of more than 3.5 percent against the dollar. The euro dipped to a low below $1.03 earlier on Friday, down just over a cent from its seven-week peak at $1.0414 set on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro received a slight fillip after European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Vice President Christian Noyer said in an interview with an Italian newspaper that euro zone economies could grow by more than three percent in 2000 and could see U.S.-style growth if governments implemented structural reforms. ((London capital markets, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw1705ghr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
