\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hits 7-week high vs dollar, eyes Dow. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2000\par 14:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 5 (Reuters) - Jitters about further weakness in U.S. stocks coupled with growing optimism about the euro zone's economic recovery buoyed the euro to seven-week highs against the dollar on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Unwinding of Y2K related safe-haven trades, which saw funds flowing into the dollar last year as it was perceived to be a safer bet than the euro, also bolstered the euro. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its key interest rates unchanged, as expected, following its first meeting of 2000 on Wednesday. But traders said that an interest rate hike in the first quarter was imminent given the strong economic backdrop. \par \par \uc2  "I think the euro can gain more, to $1.05 or above, as there are more Y2K trades to be unwound and nervousness about the Federal Reserve's decision in February will cap stocks," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank in London. "The German (unemployment) data was also promising, giving the euro a boost as well." \par \par \uc2  The euro climbed to $1.0402, its highest since November 18 and a gain of 3-1/2 cents from this year's lows set on Monday, on fears that the Dow Jones Industrial Average, which tumbled more than three percent on Tuesday, could be in for a major correction after a decade-long bull run. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said falling U.S. stocks and bonds reflected worries the U.S. Federal Reserve could raise interest rates when it meets in February. \par \par \uc2  EURO RECUPERATING \par \par \uc2  But the euro pulled back against the dollar to the mid-$1.03s by 1250 GMT, on the back of a recovery in S&P futures, which indicated a stronger Dow opening, traders said. March S&P futures were up 5.60 points. \par \par \uc2  "I would say the euro is recuperating. There may be people who are being cautious. The dollar is mainly being affected by a fall in stocks. It's not so much a euro appreciation as it is a fall in the dollar, with the Nasdaq and Dow down," said a trader at a German bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The German jobless total fell by a seasonally adjusted 68,000 in December from the previous month, though the unemployment rate ticked up to 10.3 percent from 10.0 percent in November. German Federal Labour Office President Bernhard Jagoda said he expected the jobless total to fall further in January. \par \par \uc2  German and Eurozone service sector surveys showed the sectors continued to expand in December, but at a slower pace than previously. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen, meanwhile, rose to three-week highs above 103.50, boosted by gains in the euro EURJPY=) to six-week highs against the yen around 107.50, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The impact of the Dow sell-off was also somewhat offset by a 2.42-percent drop in the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average, they added. \par \par \uc2  Wariness to sell dollars too aggressively also persisted after Tuesday's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had pushed the dollar up to the 103-area after a test of four-year lows near 101 set last month. \par \par \uc2  Although bullishness on the Nikkei has been cited as a major reason for the yen's ascent since mid-1999, the impact of Wednesday's selloff was diluted by an apparent lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly related to it, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could be supported if foreign investors sell Tokyo stocks and buy dollars to repatriate their funds, but it is unclear yet whether such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are taking place," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  If anything, dealers said the Nikkei's pullback could be seen as a bargain-hunting opportunity by overseas investors. \par \par \uc2  But senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi earlier on Wednesday reiterated the yen's rise since last summer was undesirable and the ministry would take appropriate action as needed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw1507z00\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
