\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro extends rally as U.S. stocks drop. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2000\par 22:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 4 (Reuters) - The euro extended its birthday rally on Tuesday, the second trading day of 2000 when dealers welcomed signs of stronger growth in Europe and worried about Wall Street's health as U.S. stocks tumbled for a second day. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, positive European manufacturing data and tumbling U.S. asset markets sparked the euro's rally which continued overnight. \par \par \uc2  By late afternoon, the one-year old euro commanded a nearly 2-1/4 percent gain on the yen, more than half a percent gain against the British pound and a 0.37 percent against the dollar. In the two trading days of 2000, the adolescent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained 2.33 percent in value against the dollar and 3.12 percent against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "We think the lows have been seen for the euro," said Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York. "You have both improving cyclical conditions in the euro area and the risk in U.S. asset prices pushing the euro higher." \par \par \uc2  The selling spree on Wall Street continued on Tuesday against a background of rising U.S. bond yields and fears the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates more aggressively than previously expected to help cool the red-hot economy. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average tumbled 3.17 percent, falling nearly 360 points to 10997.93 while the technology laden NASDAQ index dropped 5.56 percent to 3901.49. \par \par \uc2  Overnight the euro received a small boost when Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again sold its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the open market in order to prevent a rapidly strengthening yen from choking off the nation's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  For the third time since November, the Bank of Japan acted to block the yen's rise to 101.00 to the dollar when the yen hit a four-year high of 101.25. In late trading, the dollar stood at 103.27 yen, up over 1-1/2 percent. \par \par \uc2  "This looks like a gentle reminder from the Japanese authorities that they're really uncomfortable with the level of the exchange rate Citibank's Sinche said. \par \par \uc2  Fears there may be more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the days before a meeting in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Group of Seven rich nations kept the yen wedged in a relatively tight trading range. \par \par \uc2  While traders and analysts cheered the euro's new gains, many questioned the rally's durability, noting there may be big obstacles to more gains above the $1.0450 level. \par \par \uc2  "The jury is still out on growth in Europe and that is why the euro came off a little from its session highs," at $1.0343 explained Banc One Capital Markets vice president Ralph DelZenero in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  "We've come two-and-a half, almost three cents in the last day-and-a-half, so we've seen a pretty significant move, and I think that we've just run out of steam a little bit," added Bob Houck, trader at Norwest Bank in Minneapolis. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets shrugged off news that President Bill Clinton nominated Alan Greenspan to a fourth term as chairman of the Federal Reserve Board, a move which dealers said had been widely expected and should be positive for the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 2125GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %  1999\par \f28 \par