\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar vulnerable to Dow, problem for U.S., Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2000\par 16:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 4 (Reuters) - The dollar's Achilles' heel lies exposed early in 2000 as the buffeting U.S. stocks suffered in the first two days of the New Year raises the risks that foreign investors may pull money out of the United States. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's vulnerability is causing headaches abroad too, especially in Japan, which is desperately trying to keep a lid on its rising yen to prevent it snuffing out a nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen on the foreign exchanges on Tuesday for the second time in two weeks as the dollar fell across the board and threatened to retest four-year lows against the yen. "If the dollar falls because the U.S. asset market is going down and there is a downturn in U.S. growth, it would be bad for everybody," said Paul Lambert, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "It might be more of a headache for Japan because they are more export-orientated than the euro zone and the U.S. is a more important export market for them." \par \par \uc2  More \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be seen if the dollar falls further against the yen, analysts said, and the chances of the first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 18 months by both the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rise. \par \par \uc2  CAPITAL FLIGHT FROM U.S. TO HAMPER BOJ \par \par \uc2  Speculation that a decade-long rally in U.S. stocks could be nearing an end is expected to mount if broad equity market indices like the Dow Jones Industrial Average extend losses they have suffered amid rising U.S. long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Dow fell more than one percent on Monday and was last down 0.8 percent on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Capital flight out of the United States, which needs to fund an enormous and widening current account deficit with foreign money, risks sending the dollar into a tailspin and would hinder Japan's attempts to stem industry-crippling yen gains. \par \par \uc2  "The current account deficit is the issue that is really going to pull the dollar down as it could become harder and harder for the U.S. to finance this debt in such an environment," said Martin Squires, head of research at Rudolf Wolff in London. \par \par \uc2  Net foreign purchases of U.S. equities totalled $43.8 billion in 1998 and about $29 billion in the second quarter of 1999 alone, according to data published in December by Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  The size of Japanese holdings of U.S. assets makes the dollar particularly vulnerable against the yen if U.S. stocks and bonds keep falling, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Merrill Lynch in December said Japan bought 938.5 billion yen worth of U.S. bonds and 715.5 billion yen worth of U.S. stocks between April and September 1999. \par \par \uc2  The yen is already expected to be boosted by Japanese money returning home before the fiscal year ends in March. Independent dollar weakness would only enhancing the yen's prowess by fuelling such Japanese capital repatriation. \par \par \uc2  This, together with the current account surplus Japan is running, risk strengthening the yen to the point at which the country's recovery could be jeopardised. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said this could explain Japan's desire to prevent the dollar from tumbling below the four-year trough of 101.22 yen it hit in November 26. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has been in three times around these levels so it is clear there is a stand-off and that it is a line they want to defend," said Johnathan Bayley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Paribas in Paris. \par \par \uc2  However, for the time being, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are wary of betting on a slump in the U.S. stock market, which surprised them with its resilience to the 1997 Asian crisis and the Russia-triggered turmoil in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe it will be the end of the world for U.S. stocks but we have heard that so many times and there has always been someone else to buy it, so it is just too early to say that the dollar is going to tank," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in London. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw14082bz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
